{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8baae4da3e916972", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tSearching for Sound-Meaning Collisions: Graph-Based\n2\tAffordance Retrieval and Multi-Evaluator Ranking for Pun\n3\tTranslation at CLEF 2026 JOKER Task 2\n4\tJoker Task 2 at CLEF 2026\n5\tRussell Taylor1,* , Adam Brikman1 and Prateek Awate1\n6\t1\n7\t\n8\tGeorgia Institute of Technology, North Ave NW, Atlanta, GA 30332\n9\t\n10\tAbstract\n11\t\n12\tFifteen years ago, Low proposed that pun translators should stop searching for equivalent words and instead search\n13\tfor new points of contact between sound and meaning. In this paper, we investigate that idea computationally.\n14\tWe model pun translation as a process of discovery, exploration, and selection. A retrieval system searches\n15\tsemantic and phonological neighborhoods for target-language affordances: sound–meaning bridges that may\n16\tsupport new wordplay. Multiple language models then explore these opportunities by generating competing\n17\ttranslations, while a multi-perspective generate-and-rank architecture selects among them.\n18\tBeyond system development, our primary contribution is an analysis of how retrieved affordances propagate\n19\tthrough the translation process. We find that generators actively exploit retrieved opportunities, evaluators\n20\tprogressively concentrate around stronger sound–meaning bridges, and exact phonological collisions are selected\n21\tat disproportionately high rates when available. At the same time, many puns still yield no usable affordances,\n22\tsuggesting that retrieval remains the central bottleneck in computational pun translation.\n23\tThe resulting picture is remarkably close to the process envisioned by Low. Successful pun translation emerges\n24\tnot from preserving source-language words, but from discovering new places in the target language where sound\n25\tand meaning collide.\n26\t\n27\tKeywords\n28\t\n29\tPun translation, Computational humor, Wordplay generation, Retrieval augmented generation\n30\t\n31\t1. Introduction\n32\tPun translation is often described as impossible because the linguistic accident that creates a pun in\n33\tone language rarely survives translation into another. Yet translators have long known that successful\n34\tpun translation does not require preserving the original word. It requires finding another place in the\n35\ttarget language where sound and meaning collide.\n36\tOne particularly useful perspective was proposed by Low, who argued that translators should not\n37\tsearch for equivalent words, but for new points of contact between sound and meaning. His squares,\n38\tpentagons, and hexagons describe a progressively expanding search through semantic and phonological\n39\tneighborhoods in pursuit of such opportunities. Although developed as a practical framework for\n40\tprofessional human translators, the underlying process resembles a search procedure that lends itself\n41\tnaturally to computational implementation [1].\n42\tOur 2025 CLEF Joker Task 2 submissions, which achieved first and second place in human evaluation,\n43\twere heavily influenced by this perspective. We combined semantic and phonetic retrieval with multiagent evaluation and found that systems optimized for humor often outperformed approaches focused\n44\ton literal translation [2]. However, an important question remained unanswered: what role does\n45\tretrieval actually play in successful pun translation?\n46\tIn this work we treat pun translation as a process of discovery, exploration, and selection. Retrieval\n47\tidentifies semantic–phonetic affordances: target-language opportunities where sound and meaning may\n48\tCLEF 2026 Working Notes, 21 – 24 September 2026, Jena, Germany\n49\tCorresponding author.\n50\t$ rdtaylorjr@gatech.edu (R. Taylor); abrikman3@gatech.edu (A. Brikman); pawate3@gatech.edu (P. Awate)\n51\t\u001a 0009-0007-0702-2375 (R. Taylor); 0000-0000-0000-0000 (A. Brikman); 0009-0002-1878-9700 (P. Awate)\n52\t\n53\t*\n54\t\n55\t© 2026 Copyright for this paper by its authors. Use permitted under Creative Commons License Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0).\n56\t\n57\t\fsupport new wordplay [3]. Generators explore these opportunities by producing multiple competing\n58\ttranslations. Evaluators then select among them using multiple perspectives reflecting humor, wordplay\n59\tquality, fluency, and preservation of the joke’s underlying setup and humorous intent.\n60\tTo support this investigation, we substantially expand the retrieval space through phrase-level\n61\tsemantic embeddings and learned phonetic embeddings, replace regeneration-based evaluation with\n62\ta two-stage generate-and-rank architecture, and trace retrieved affordances throughout the entire\n63\ttranslation pipeline. This allows us not only to improve performance, but also to study which sound–\n64\tmeaning bridges survive successive stages of generation and selection.\n65\tThe results suggest that successful pun translation is neither direct translation nor unconstrained\n66\tcreativity. It is a search process. Retrieval uncovers hidden bridges between semantic domains, generators explore them, and evaluators progressively concentrate around the strongest collisions between\n67\tsound and meaning.\n68\t\n69\t2. System Improvements\n70\t2.1. Preprocessing\n71\tConsistent with Miller’s proposed computational framework for pun translation, preprocessing aims to\n72\tidentify the source pun and its associated semantic domains before retrieval and candidate generation\n73\t[4].\n74\t2.1.1. Data Cleaning\n75\tWe revised our preprocessing pipeline to improve LLM comprehension of the source puns [2]. Compared\n76\tto our previous system, the cleaner normalizes Unicode punctuation, repairs malformed quotation and\n77\tdialogue structures, preserves lexical hashtag cues that explicitly mark pun words, removes socialmedia artifacts (URLs, handles, hashtags, emojis, and reaction text), and incorporates approximately\n78\t100 dataset-specific repairs identified through automated validation. These changes were intended to\n79\timprove downstream pun identification and interpretation rather than contribute directly to translation\n80\tquality.\n81\t2.1.2. Pun Word Identification and Translation\n82\tWe retained the pun word identification and translation stage from our previous system but simplified\n83\tthe output representation. The model identifies the pun word, pun type, and two semantic domain\n84\tword lists associated with the wordplay. Unlike the previous system, we removed explicit context-word\n85\textraction and translate only the semantic fields required for downstream retrieval. We implemented\n86\tthe pipeline using gemini-3-flash and structured JSON outputs.\n87\t\n88\t2.2. Expanded Affordance Embedding Spaces\n89\tA limitation of our previous system was that affordance discovery operated primarily at the word level.\n90\tMany puns, however, rely on fixed expressions, collocations, and phrase-level reinterpretations that\n91\textend beyond individual lexical items. To address this limitation, we constructed a 370,450-entry\n92\tFrench expression bank and indexed it for dense retrieval.\n93\t2.2.1. Expression-Level Semantic Embeddings\n94\tThe expression bank combines 51,589 French Wiktionary entries labeled as expressions, proverbs, or\n95\tlocutions [5]; 12,308 PARSEME-derived sentence contexts containing annotated multiword expressions\n96\t[6]; and 306,553 high-association OpenSubtitles collocations [7] mined using spaCy lemmatization,\n97\tpart-of-speech pattern filtering, frequency thresholds, and pointwise mutual information scoring [8].\n98\tAfter normalization and deduplication, 355,803 entries (96.1%) consisted of multi-word units.\n99\t\n100\t\fAll entries were embedded using BAAI/bge-m3 [9] with normalized embeddings and indexed using\n101\tFAISS inner-product search [10]. Unlike the previous system, which primarily searched for individual\n102\tlexical affordances, the expression bank enables retrieval over multi-word expressions, collocations,\n103\tidiomatic constructions, and longer semantic contexts. These additions expand the search space available\n104\tto the retrieval system and provide phrase-level semantic anchors capable of supporting target-language\n105\twordplay.\n106\t2.2.2. Learned Phonetic Embeddings\n107\tStarting from a French lexicon containing orthographic forms and IPA transcriptions, we constructed\n108\ta large-scale phonetic relevance graph containing 245,746 unique word–IPA pairs and 4.46 million\n109\tlabeled phonological relations [11]. Positive relations included exact homophones, near-homophones,\n110\tschwa-deletion variants, rhotic variants, nasalization variants, rhyme relations, consonant-skeleton\n111\trelations, and vowel-skeleton relations; hard negatives were mined from phonologically similar forms\n112\tthat lacked meaningful phonological equivalence. The resulting dataset yielded 2.72 million positive\n113\ttraining pairs and 355,007 held-out evaluation relations. Relation counts are summarized in Table 1.\n114\tTable 1\n115\tPositive relation types used for phonetic embedding.\n116\tRelation type\n117\t\n118\tCount\n119\t\n120\tStrong rhyme\n121\tVowel skeleton\n122\tConsonant skeleton\n123\tRhotic variant\n124\tNear homophone (edit distance 2)\n125\tNasalization variant\n126\tExact homophone\n127\tSchwa deletion\n128\tWeak rhyme\n129\tNear homophone (edit distance 1)\n130\tBoundary deletion\n131\t\n132\t1,518,818\n133\t426,532\n134\t421,482\n135\t73,139\n136\t66,379\n137\t57,013\n138\t56,521\n139\t30,295\n140\t25,761\n141\t8,198\n142\t323\n143\t\n144\tTotal\n145\t\n146\t2,684,461\n147\t\n148\tUnlike traditional phonetic retrieval systems that focus primarily on exact pronunciation similarity, the supervision signal intentionally emphasizes broader phonological neighborhoods, including\n149\trhyme, consonant-skeleton, and vowel-skeleton relations, to support retrieval of approximate sound\n150\tcorrespondences useful for pun construction. These relations are used to fine-tune BAAI/bge-m3 [9]\n151\ton IPA sequences using SentenceTransformers and Multiple Negatives Ranking Loss [12]. Training\n152\tpairs are balanced through capped per-relation sampling to prevent dominant relation classes from\n153\toverwhelming the embedding geometry. Training used a batch size of 128 for one epoch. The resulting\n154\tembedding space was trained to capture phonological relationships useful for pun construction rather\n155\tthan exact pronunciation equivalence alone. The trained model embeds IPA representations into a\n156\tdense retrieval space indexed with FAISS [10] and supports approximate phonetic retrieval through\n157\tcosine-similarity nearest-neighbor search.\n158\tWe evaluated the learned phonetic encoder on an intrinsic retrieval benchmark containing 6,321\n159\tquery IPA forms and 244,948 indexed items. Performance was strongest for near-homophone and\n160\tconsonant-skeleton relations, achieving Recall@10 of 0.642 and 0.371 respectively, while hard-negative\n161\tretrieval remained near zero. These results indicate that the embedding space effectively captures\n162\tpun-relevant phonetic similarity beyond exact homophone matching.\n163\t\n164\t\fTable 2\n165\tIntrinsic phonetic retrieval evaluation.\n166\tRelation\n167\t\n168\tR@10\n169\t\n170\tMRR\n171\t\n172\tAll\n173\tNear homophone (edit 1)\n174\tConsonant skeleton\n175\tStrong rhyme\n176\tHard negative\n177\t\n178\t0.394\n179\t0.642\n180\t0.371\n181\t0.027\n182\t0.000\n183\t\n184\t0.191\n185\t0.293\n186\t0.172\n187\t0.013\n188\t0.000\n189\t\n190\t2.3. Graph-Based Affordance Retrieval\n191\tFollowing Low’s proposal of pun translation as a search for exploitable sound–meaning collisions,\n192\twe model retrieval as exploration of semantic and phonetic neighborhoods connecting two semantic\n193\tdomains rather than direct translation between individual lexical items. We refer to retrieved phoneticsemantic pairs as affordances because they provide opportunities for target-language pun construction\n194\trather than direct translations.\n195\tWe view the retrieval space as a heterogeneous graph whose vertices are lexical items and expressions.\n196\tSemantic retrieval induces semantic edges, while phonetic retrieval induces phonetic edges. Affordance\n197\tdiscovery corresponds to identifying bridge structures that connect the semantic regions associated\n198\twith the two source-pun domains through combinations of semantic and phonetic relations.\n199\tLet 𝑉 denote the set of French lexical items and expressions. Semantic retrieval induces semantic\n200\tneighborhoods 𝑁𝑆 (·), while phonetic retrieval induces phonetic neighborhoods 𝑁𝑃 (·). Let 𝐴 and 𝐵\n201\tdenote the two translated lists of words representing the distinct semantic domains of the source pun\n202\tword. These serve as the seed sets for two semantic regions.\n203\t2.3.1. Semantic Expansion\n204\tEach semantic region is expanded independently through dense retrieval over the French expression\n205\tbank and bounded FastText lexical expansion [13]. The resulting semantic neighborhoods are\n206\t𝐴𝑆 = 𝑁𝑆 (𝐴),\n207\t\n208\t𝐵𝑆 = 𝑁𝑆 (𝐵).\n209\t\n210\tAll retrieved candidates must possess an attested IPA representation before participating in subsequent\n211\tphonetic retrieval. Detached phonetic recovery is defined as\n212\t𝐴𝑃 = 𝑁𝑃 (𝐴 ∪ 𝐴𝑆 ),\n213\t\n214\t𝐵𝑃 = 𝑁𝑃 (𝐵 ∪ 𝐵𝑆 ),\n215\t\n216\twhere 𝑁𝑃 denotes phonetic-neighborhood expansion in the learned IPA embedding space.\n217\t2.3.2. Bucketized Bridge Mining\n218\tWe define phonetic bridge retrieval as\n219\tΦ(𝑋, 𝑌 ) = {(𝑥, 𝑦) ∈ 𝑋 × 𝑌 : ipa(𝑥) ̸= ∅, ipa(𝑦) ̸= ∅, cos(e𝑥 , e𝑦 ) ≥ 𝜏 }\n220\twhere e𝑥 and e𝑦 are the learned phonetic embeddings of 𝑥 and 𝑦, respectively. For each bucket,\n221\tretrieval considers all candidate pairs between the source and target candidate sets and retains only\n222\tthose satisfying the phonetic similarity criterion defined by Φ.\n223\tRetrieval proceeds through six ordered stages:\n224\t1. Phonetic matches between original domain terms\n225\t𝑅1 = Φ(𝐴, 𝐵)\n226\t\n227\t\f2. Phonetic matches between semantic expansions of 𝐴 and original 𝐵 terms\n228\t𝑅2 = Φ(𝐴𝑆 , 𝐵)\n229\t3. Phonetic matches between original 𝐴 terms and semantic expansions of 𝐵\n230\t𝑅3 = Φ(𝐴, 𝐵𝑆 )\n231\t4. Phonetic matches between semantic expansions of both domains\n232\t𝑅4 = Φ(𝐴𝑆 , 𝐵𝑆 )\n233\t5. Phonetic recovery from the 𝐴 domain as the sole semantic anchor\n234\t𝑅5 = Φ(𝐴𝑃 , 𝐴 ∪ 𝐴𝑆 )\n235\t6. Phonetic recovery from the 𝐵 domain as the sole semantic anchor\n236\t𝑅6 = Φ(𝐵𝑃 , 𝐵 ∪ 𝐵𝑆 )\n237\tDuplicate affordances retain ownership of the earliest stage in which they are discovered. Phonetic\n238\tretrieval is performed through nearest-neighbor search in the learned IPA embedding space rather\n239\tthan exact homophone lookup, allowing retrieval of approximate sound relationships learned during\n240\tphonetic embedding training.\n241\t2.3.3. Affordance Ranking and Pruning\n242\tAffordance ranking combines several heuristic quality signals. Phonetic scores measure pronunciation similarity, semantic scores measure anchorability to the source semantic domains, naturalness\n243\tscores estimate recognizability as French lexical material, and pivot scores estimate usefulness for pun\n244\tconstruction. Overall scores represent the combined affordance-ranking score used during retrieval\n245\tpruning.\n246\tSeveral pruning stages are applied before export. Exact duplicates are removed. Trivial inflectional\n247\tvariants, accent variants, plural variants, and same-root morphological echoes are penalized or filtered.\n248\tPer-surface and per-root caps prevent a single lexical family from dominating the affordance list.\n249\tAdditional constraints limit identity affordances and other low-creativity collisions.\n250\tThe final retrieval output is a diversified set of higher-scoring retrieved affordances under our filtering\n251\tcriteria, distributed across the expanded retrieval space. These affordances serve as constraints and\n252\tinspiration for downstream French pun generation.\n253\t\n254\t2.4. Candidate Generation\n255\tOur previous system generated a single French pun per input row using the English source text,\n256\ttranslated semantic domains, and a small set of retrieved French affordances. However, affordance\n257\tretrieval covered only 14% of the dataset, limiting the usefulness of retrieval-guided generation.\n258\tThe expanded retrieval system increased affordance coverage to 50.8% of examples and produced\n259\tbetween one and six affordances for each successful retrieval. This provided more frequent and diverse\n260\tguidance to the generation stage.\n261\tFor claude-sonnet-4.6 and gemini-3-flash, the generator received the English pun, translated\n262\tsemantic domains, and retrieved affordances and produced twelve distinct French pun candidates. The\n263\tprompt encouraged different forms of French wordplay, including homophony, near-homophony,\n264\tpolysemy, idiomatic reinterpretation, phrase-level wordplay, and semantic compensation. Retrieved\n265\taffordances were presented as creative opportunities rather than required lexical targets, allowing the\n266\tmodel to explore multiple ways of exploiting the same underlying semantic and phonetic relationships.\n267\tFor gpt-5.5 and gemini-3.1-pro-preview, a single candidate was generated for each example\n268\tand passed directly to the ensemble stage. These runs were included to evaluate stronger frontier\n269\tmodels with a lower-cost of generation.\n270\tThis shifted generation from a single-solution paradigm to a generate-and-select paradigm, allowing\n271\tdownstream evaluators to choose among multiple realizations of the same retrieved affordance structure.\n272\t\n273\t\f2.5. Two-Stage Multi-Agent Ranking\n274\tThe 2025 system evaluated a single generated pun using multiple agents that assigned scores and\n275\ttriggered regeneration when scores fell below a threshold. In contrast, the 2026 system reformulates\n276\tevaluation as a two-stage ranking problem.\n277\t2.5.1. Stage 1: Intra-Model Candidate Selection\n278\tFor claude-sonnet-4.6 and gemini-3-flash, the generator produces twelve candidate puns for\n279\teach source example. Candidate strings are extracted, assigned deterministic five-digit identifiers,\n280\tstripped of generator metadata, and randomly shuffled before evaluation. The evaluator therefore never\n281\tobserves candidate order or model provenance.\n282\tA single judge model is prompted to rank candidates from four independent perspectives [14][15]:\n283\t• Comedian - Which candidate is funniest to a native French speaker?\n284\t• Linguist - Which candidate exhibits the strongest and most recognizable wordplay?\n285\t• Editor – Which candidate is most natural and fluent in French?\n286\t• Translator – Which candidate best preserves the humorous intent of the English source?\n287\tRather than assigning numerical scores, each persona returns an ordered top-five ranking of candidate\n288\tidentifiers. Rankings are then aggregated using weighted Borda voting [16]. Candidates omitted from\n289\ta persona’s top-five list receive zero points. The candidate with the highest aggregate Borda score is\n290\tselected as the Stage 1 winner. The output of Stage 1 is a single winning candidate for each generator\n291\tmodel.\n292\t2.5.2. Stage 2: Cross-Model Ensemble Selection\n293\tNext, the Stage 1 winners from claude-sonnet-4.6 and gemini-3-flash are combined with singlecandidate outputs generated by gpt-5.5 and gemini-3.1-pro. This produces four candidate puns for each\n294\tsource example.\n295\tAs in Stage 1, candidates are assigned fresh deterministic identifiers, shuffled, and stripped of sourcemodel metadata before evaluation. The same four personas independently rank all four candidates.\n296\tPersona rankings are again aggregated using weighted Borda voting to produce a final ensemble winner.\n297\tThe ranking pipeline is executed using gpt-5.5, claude-sonnet-4.6, and gemini-3.1-pro as\n298\tjudge models. We evaluated multiple aggregation strategies:\n299\t1. Judges then models - Aggregate persona rankings within each judge model, then aggregate\n300\tacross judge models.\n301\t2. Models then judges - Aggregate judge-model rankings first, then aggregate persona rankings.\n302\t3. Pooled rankings - Aggregate all rankings simultaneously within a single Borda computation.\n303\tJudge-model weights and persona weights are independently configurable, allowing ensemble variants\n304\tto emphasize different evaluation criteria. We additionally measure inter-judge agreement, promptposition exposure effects, and model self-preference using normal, half-weight, and removed-self-vote\n305\tcorrection schemes.\n306\t\n307\t3. Results\n308\t3.1. Retrieval Statistics\n309\tThe retrieval system generated 3,748 affordances for 2,064 of 4,061 source puns, corresponding to 50.8%\n310\tcoverage (Table 3). Covered source puns contained an average of 1.82 affordances, while the overall\n311\taverage was 0.92 affordances per source pun. Coverage was slightly higher for homophonic puns (55.1%)\n312\tthan for homographic puns (50.2%).\n313\t\n314\t\fDirect 𝐴𝑆 –𝐵𝑆 bridges accounted for 2,252 retrieved affordances (60.1% of the total). Detached\n315\tphonetic recovery contributed the remaining 1,496 affordances, split between 𝐴𝑃 -anchored retrieval\n316\t(719 affordances; 19.2%) and 𝐵𝑃 -anchored retrieval (777 affordances; 20.7%) (Table 3).\n317\tTable 3\n318\tRetrieval output by bucket.\n319\tRetrieval bucket\n320\t\n321\tSource puns\n322\t\n323\tAffordances\n324\t\n325\tMean score\n326\t\n327\t𝐴𝑆 –𝐵𝑆 bridge\n328\t𝐴𝑃 -anchored retrieval\n329\t𝐵𝑃 -anchored retrieval\n330\t\n331\t1,287 (31.7%)\n332\t556 (13.7%)\n333\t587 (14.5%)\n334\t\n335\t2,252 (60.1%)\n336\t719 (19.2%)\n337\t777 (20.7%)\n338\t\n339\t0.711\n340\t0.626\n341\t0.626\n342\t\n343\tTotal\n344\t\n345\t2,064 (50.8%)\n346\t\n347\t3,748 (100.0%)\n348\t\n349\t0.677\n350\t\n351\tThe retrieval inventory was dominated by near-homophonic bridges. Similar-sound relations accounted for 3,344 affordances (89.2%), while same-sound relations accounted for 404 affordances (10.8%).\n352\tSame-sound affordances achieved higher mean overall scores than similar-sound affordances (0.817\n353\tvs. 0.660). Likewise, bridges discovered between 𝐴𝑆 and 𝐵𝑆 achieved higher mean overall scores than\n354\tdetached recovery affordances (0.711 vs. 0.626).\n355\tThe affordance count distribution was strongly skewed toward small candidate sets (Table 4). Nearly\n356\thalf of all source puns produced no affordances, while 41.2% produced one or two affordances. Only\n357\t9.6% of source puns produced three or more affordances.\n358\tTable 4\n359\tDistribution of retrieved affordances per source pun.\n360\tRetrieved affordances\n361\t\n362\tSource puns\n363\t\n364\t0\n365\t1\n366\t2\n367\t3-6\n368\t\n369\t1,997 (49.2%)\n370\t956 (23.5%)\n371\t717 (17.7%)\n372\t391 (9.6%)\n373\t\n374\tTotal\n375\t\n376\t4,061 (100.0%)\n377\t\n378\t3.2. Affordance Utilization\n379\tTables 5 and 6 summarize affordance retention and quality across retrieval, generation, and selection\n380\tstages. Successive selection stages reduced the number of represented affordances while increasing\n381\ttheir mean quality scores.\n382\tTable 5\n383\tAffordance retention across retrieval, generation, and selection stages.\n384\tStage\n385\tAffordance retrieval\n386\tStage 1 generation\n387\tStage 1 selection\n388\tStage 2 finalists\n389\tStage 2 winners\n390\t\n391\tPuns using affordances\n392\t\n393\tDistinct affordances used\n394\t\n395\t2,064 (50.8%)\n396\t1,935 (47.6%)\n397\t1,167 (28.7%)\n398\t443 (10.9%)\n399\t141 (3.5%)\n400\t\n401\t3,748 (100.0%)\n402\t3,335 (89.5%)\n403\t1,832 (49.1%)\n404\t650 (17.4%)\n405\t206 (5.5%)\n406\t\n407\tAffordance usage remained dominated by 𝐴𝑆 –𝐵𝑆 bridges throughout the pipeline. These accounted\n408\tfor 60.1% of retrieved affordances, 71.8% and 68.7% of affordance usage in claude-sonnet-4.6 and\n409\tgemini-3-flash candidates, 73.1% and 70.4% after Stage 1 selection, and 72.7% and 72.5% among\n410\tStage 2 finalists and winners.\n411\t\n412\t\fTable 6\n413\tMean quality scores for affordances used across retrieval, generation, and selection stages. Higher values indicate\n414\tstronger affordances.\n415\tStage\n416\t\n417\tOverall\n418\t\n419\tPhonetic\n420\t\n421\tNaturalness\n422\t\n423\tSemantic\n424\t\n425\tPivot\n426\t\n427\tAffordance retrieval\n428\tStage 1 generation\n429\tStage 1 selection\n430\tStage 2 finalists\n431\tStage 2 winners\n432\t\n433\t0.677\n434\t0.702\n435\t0.713\n436\t0.716\n437\t0.724\n438\t\n439\t0.846\n440\t0.858\n441\t0.866\n442\t0.866\n443\t0.873\n444\t\n445\t0.520\n446\t0.556\n447\t0.572\n448\t0.578\n449\t0.588\n450\t\n451\t0.892\n452\t0.941\n453\t0.954\n454\t0.968\n455\t0.978\n456\t\n457\t0.520\n458\t0.562\n459\t0.579\n460\t0.585\n461\t0.596\n462\t\n463\tIn contrast, same-sound affordances were consistently overrepresented relative to their prevalence in\n464\tthe retrieval inventory. Although they comprised only 10.8% of retrieved affordances, they accounted\n465\tfor 17.6% and 14.9% of affordance usage in claude-sonnet-4.6 and gemini-3-flash candidates,\n466\t22.9% and 19.4% after Stage 1 selection, and 23.8% and 27.5% among Stage 2 finalists and winners.\n467\tAmong candidates that used retrieval, claude-sonnet-4.6 candidates employed an average of\n468\t1.38 affordances and gemini-3-flash candidates employed an average of 1.36 affordances. Ranked\n469\tcandidates showed similar values (1.43 and 1.39, respectively). Most affordance usage involved only\n470\tone side of a retrieved bridge. In Claude-generated candidates, 48.4% of matches used only the left\n471\taffordance, 25.0% used only the right affordance, and 26.6% used both bridge endpoints; the corresponding proportions for gemini-3-flash were similar. Candidates that used retrieval employed 1.38\n472\taffordances on average for claude-sonnet-4.6 and 1.36 for gemini-3-flash, increasing to 1.43\n473\tand 1.39 after Stage 1 selection.\n474\t\n475\t3.3. Candidate Selection Results\n476\tThe judge personas exhibited substantial diversity in their preferred candidates. Complete agreement occurred for only 16.8% of Claude-generated examples and 5.4% of Gemini-generated examples. Claude examples produced an average of 2.29 distinct persona winners, compared with 2.63 for gemini-3-flash.\n477\tMore than half of gemini-3-flash examples (56.9%) and 38.7% of claude-sonnet-4.6 examples\n478\tproduced at least three distinct persona winners, indicating that different personas frequently prioritized\n479\tdifferent candidate qualities. This diversity of preferences motivates the use of rank aggregation rather\n480\tthan relying on a single evaluation perspective.\n481\t\n482\t3.4. Ensemble Results\n483\tDiscriminator models exhibited markedly different selection preferences. The gemini-3.1-pro judge\n484\tselected Gemini-family candidates 72.4% of the time, the gpt-5.5 judge selected GPT-generated\n485\tcandidates 32.6% of the time, and the claude-sonnet-4.6 judge selected Claude-generated candidates\n486\tas its top-ranked choice 27.1% of the time. As shown in Table 8, discriminator choice and persona\n487\tweighting both substantially affected the final winner distribution, with gemini-3.1-pro judging\n488\tstrongly self-biased and translator-only weighting increasing the share of gpt-5.5 winners.\n489\tTable 7\n490\tGenerator sources selected under different Stage 2 aggregation orders.\n491\tAggregation\n492\t\n493\tgpt-5.5\n494\t\n495\tgemini-3.1-pro\n496\t\n497\tclaude-sonnet-4.6\n498\t\n499\tgemini-3-flash\n500\t\n501\tJudges-first\n502\tModels-first\n503\tPooled\n504\t\n505\t1,165\n506\t1,161\n507\t1,176\n508\t\n509\t1,954\n510\t1,993\n511\t2,066\n512\t\n513\t507\n514\t463\n515\t459\n516\t\n517\t435\n518\t444\n519\t360\n520\t\n521\tPersona disagreement remained common throughout cross-model selection. Complete persona\n522\tagreement on the top candidate occurred in only 10.6% for gpt-5.5 judging, 25% for gemini-3.1-pro\n523\t\n524\t\fjudging, and 18.0% of examples for claude-sonnet-4.6 judging. Most examples produced two or\n525\tthree distinct persona winners. Despite disagreement at rank one, personas generally ranked similar\n526\tcandidate sets highly, with all four finalists appearing in every persona’s top-four ranking. Pairwise\n527\tKendall rank correlations ranged from 0.56 to 0.86 depending on the persona pair [17].\n528\tTable 8\n529\tStage 2 winner distributions under selected persona-weighting and discriminator-model configurations. Entries\n530\treport final winner counts by generator model, with percentages of all 4,061 puns.\n531\tPersona weights\n532\t\n533\tDiscriminator models\n534\t\n535\tgpt-5.5\n536\t\n537\tgemini-3.1-pro\n538\t\n539\tclaude-sonnet-4.6\n540\t\n541\tgemini-3-flash\n542\t\n543\t25/25/25/25\n544\t45/30/25/10\n545\tComedian\n546\tLinguist\n547\tEditor\n548\tTranslator\n549\t\n550\tAll (pooled)\n551\tAll (pooled)\n552\tAll (pooled)\n553\tAll (pooled)\n554\tAll (pooled)\n555\tAll (pooled)\n556\t\n557\t1,176 (29.0%)\n558\t1,003 (24.7%)\n559\t944 (23.2%)\n560\t1,053 (25.9%)\n561\t810 (19.9%)\n562\t1,735 (42.7%)\n563\t\n564\t2,066 (50.9%)\n565\t1,847 (45.5%)\n566\t1,549 (38.1%)\n567\t1,623 (40.0%)\n568\t1,886 (46.4%)\n569\t1,793 (44.2%)\n570\t\n571\t459 (11.3%)\n572\t517 (12.7%)\n573\t678 (16.7%)\n574\t471 (11.6%)\n575\t626 (15.4%)\n576\t459 (11.3%)\n577\t\n578\t360 (8.9%)\n579\t694 (17.1%)\n580\t890 (21.9%)\n581\t914 (22.5%)\n582\t739 (18.2%)\n583\t74 (1.8%)\n584\t\n585\t25/25/25/25\n586\t45/30/25/10\n587\tComedian\n588\tLinguist\n589\tEditor\n590\tTranslator\n591\t\n592\tgpt-5.5\n593\tgpt-5.5\n594\tgpt-5.5\n595\tgpt-5.5\n596\tgpt-5.5\n597\tgpt-5.5\n598\t\n599\t1,418 (34.9%)\n600\t1,166 (28.7%)\n601\t1,101 (27.1%)\n602\t1,175 (28.9%)\n603\t888 (21.9%)\n604\t2,126 (52.4%)\n605\t\n606\t1,630 (40.1%)\n607\t1,441 (35.5%)\n608\t1,310 (32.3%)\n609\t1,404 (34.6%)\n610\t1,602 (39.4%)\n611\t1,409 (34.7%)\n612\t\n613\t483 (11.9%)\n614\t541 (13.3%)\n615\t656 (16.2%)\n616\t461 (11.4%)\n617\t663 (16.3%)\n618\t462 (11.4%)\n619\t\n620\t530 (13.1%)\n621\t913 (22.5%)\n622\t994 (24.5%)\n623\t1,021 (25.1%)\n624\t908 (22.4%)\n625\t64 (1.6%)\n626\t\n627\t25/25/25/25\n628\t45/30/25/10\n629\tComedian\n630\tLinguist\n631\tEditor\n632\tTranslator\n633\t\n634\tgemini-3.1-pro\n635\tgemini-3.1-pro\n636\tgemini-3.1-pro\n637\tgemini-3.1-pro\n638\tgemini-3.1-pro\n639\tgemini-3.1-pro\n640\t\n641\t737 (18.1%)\n642\t637 (15.7%)\n643\t640 (15.8%)\n644\t668 (16.4%)\n645\t622 (15.3%)\n646\t1,188 (29.3%)\n647\t\n648\t2,638 (65.0%)\n649\t2,358 (58.1%)\n650\t2,121 (52.2%)\n651\t2,123 (52.3%)\n652\t2,290 (56.4%)\n653\t2,476 (61.0%)\n654\t\n655\t267 (6.6%)\n656\t303 (7.5%)\n657\t409 (10.1%)\n658\t304 (7.5%)\n659\t374 (9.2%)\n660\t300 (7.4%)\n661\t\n662\t419 (10.3%)\n663\t763 (18.8%)\n664\t891 (21.9%)\n665\t966 (23.8%)\n666\t775 (19.1%)\n667\t97 (2.4%)\n668\t\n669\t25/25/25/25\n670\t45/30/25/10\n671\tComedian\n672\tLinguist\n673\tEditor\n674\tTranslator\n675\t\n676\tclaude-sonnet-4.6\n677\tclaude-sonnet-4.6\n678\tclaude-sonnet-4.6\n679\tclaude-sonnet-4.6\n680\tclaude-sonnet-4.6\n681\tclaude-sonnet-4.6\n682\t\n683\t1,238 (30.5%)\n684\t1,048 (25.8%)\n685\t890 (21.9%)\n686\t1,191 (29.3%)\n687\t950 (23.4%)\n688\t1,450 (35.7%)\n689\t\n690\t1,179 (29.0%)\n691\t1,026 (25.3%)\n692\t868 (21.4%)\n693\t873 (21.5%)\n694\t1,301 (32.0%)\n695\t1,391 (34.3%)\n696\t\n697\t1,100 (27.1%)\n698\t1,192 (29.4%)\n699\t1,364 (33.6%)\n700\t1,013 (24.9%)\n701\t1,059 (26.1%)\n702\t979 (24.1%)\n703\t\n704\t544 (13.4%)\n705\t795 (19.6%)\n706\t939 (23.1%)\n707\t984 (24.2%)\n708\t751 (18.5%)\n709\t241 (5.9%)\n710\t\n711\t3.5. Shared Task Results\n712\tAt the time of writing, our best submission is ranked first on the public leaderboard with a score of\n713\t37.783. Our strongest single-model system is ranked second (37.119), while the best all-model translator\n714\tensemble is ranked third (35.949).\n715\tThe leaderboard ablations show three clear trends. First, GPT-based generation outperformed\n716\tthe other generator families: gpt-5.5 single (37.119) exceeded gemini-3.1-pro single (30.250),\n717\tclaude-sonnet-4.6 translator (29.547), and gemini-3-flash translator (23.029). Second, translatorfocused selection consistently outperformed other persona weightings. The best translator ensemble\n718\tachieved 37.783, compared with 25.761 for editor-only, 24.161 for pun-expert-only, and 24.044 for\n719\tcomedian-only selection. Third, candidate pooling was beneficial only when combined with translatororiented ranking. The all-pooled translator ensemble achieved 35.949, compared with 28.884 for the\n720\tequal-weight ensemble, 28.493 for judges-first aggregation, and 28.259 for models-first aggregation.\n721\tEnsemble weighting also had a large effect. Translator-focused weighting outperformed comedianand pun-focused weighting schemes. This result is consistent with our 2025 findings, where systems\n722\toptimized for semantic fidelity achieved higher BLEU and BERTScore despite often producing weaker\n723\ttarget-language wordplay. The official evaluation metric therefore appears to reward preservation\n724\tof source meaning more strongly than creative pun generation, creating a potential tension between\n725\tleaderboard optimization and humor quality.\n726\tHuman evaluation, which is the primary target of our system design, has not yet been performed,\n727\tand not all participating submissions are visible on the leaderboard. The camera-ready version will\n728\treport final leaderboard standings and official human evaluation results.\n729\t\n730\t\f4. Discussion\n731\t4.1. Retrieval actually matters\n732\tRetrieved affordances were actively exploited by generators and progressively concentrated among\n733\tselected winners. As affordance diversity decreased across successive pipeline stages, the mean quality\n734\tof surviving affordances increased. This suggests that generation and ranking preferentially retained\n735\tstronger semantic–phonetic collisions rather than treating all retrieved affordances equally.\n736\t\n737\t4.2. Retrieval is still the bottleneck\n738\tDespite tripling retrieval coverage, nearly half of source puns still yielded no affordances, and retrieved\n739\taffordances appeared in only 3.5% of final ensemble winners. This highlights the central challenge\n740\tof retrieval-guided pun translation: useful sound–meaning collisions are both rare and difficult to\n741\tdiscover. Rather than serving as a pervasive generation mechanism, affordances functioned as sparse\n742\tbut valuable opportunities that were exploited only when the retrieval system uncovered particularly\n743\tstrong semantic–phonetic bridges. This difficulty mirrors the challenges faced by human translators,\n744\twho frequently abandon source-side wordplay when no suitable target-language collision exists.\n745\t\n746\t4.3. Same-sound affordances are disproportionately successful\n747\tAlthough exact sound matches constituted only a small fraction of retrieved affordances, they became\n748\tincreasingly overrepresented among selected candidates. Approximate phonetic similarity appears\n749\tvaluable for exploration, but exact phonological collisions are selected at disproportionately high rates\n750\twhen available. This pattern suggests that successful target-language wordplay remains concentrated\n751\taround rare exact sound correspondences, which provide particularly strong and easily recoverable\n752\topportunities for humorous reinterpretation.\n753\t\n754\t4.4. Affordances cluster around a small number of strong bridges\n755\tAlthough retrieval produced thousands of affordances, many represented variations of the same underlying semantic–phonetic opportunity. Successful pun translation therefore appears to depend less on the\n756\ttotal number of retrieved affordances than on the number of distinct semantic–phonetic bridges available for exploitation. Future work should focus on exploring more distant semantic and phonological\n757\tneighborhoods capable of surfacing genuinely novel opportunities for wordplay [18][19].\n758\t\n759\t4.5. The persistence of position bias\n760\tCandidate order influenced selection behavior even after randomization, indicating that current LLM\n761\tjudges are not fully position invariant. Reducing these effects will likely require repeated evaluation\n762\tunder multiple candidate orderings, albeit at increased computational cost. The persistence of position\n763\teffects despite randomization suggests that ranking variance remains a significant source of uncertainty\n764\tin LLM-based evaluation pipelines.\n765\t\n766\t4.6. Healthy disagreement is a good thing\n767\tStrong disagreement between judge personas indicates that pun quality is inherently multi-objective.\n768\tHumor, wordplay strength, fluency, and translation fidelity frequently favored different candidates,\n769\tsuggesting that multi-perspective evaluation captures aspects of quality that would be lost under a\n770\tsingle evaluator objective.\n771\tSimilarly, Gemini-3.1-pro exhibited substantial self-preference, whereas the other judges displayed\n772\tweaker generator-specific preferences. Multi-judge aggregation therefore reduces reliance on any single\n773\tdiscriminator’s biases.\n774\t\n775\t\f4.7. What the leaderboard is actually rewarding\n776\tTranslator-oriented ranking strategies substantially outperformed comedian-, editor-, and pun-focused\n777\talternatives on the public leaderboard. This large performance gap suggests that current automatic\n778\tmetrics underweight the qualities that make target-language puns entertaining to human readers\n779\tand instead place greater emphasis on preservation of source meaning. Notably, the strongest singlemodel result was obtained by a single gpt-5.5 generation rather than a generate-and-select pipeline,\n780\tsuggesting that generator capability remains a major determinant of leaderboard performance. Human\n781\tevaluation will therefore be necessary to determine whether leaderboard gains correspond to genuine\n782\timprovements in translated wordplay.\n783\t\n784\t5. Conclusion\n785\tOur analyses suggest that successful pun translation emerges through a process of discovery, exploration,\n786\tand selection. Retrieval uncovers semantic–phonetic affordances, generators explore the opportunities\n787\tthey create, and evaluators progressively concentrate around the strongest sound–meaning collisions.\n788\tThis picture is remarkably close to the account proposed by Low, who argued that translators need\n789\tnot search for equivalent words, but for new points of contact between meaning and sound. In that\n790\tsense, the most satisfying outcome of this work is not any particular score or ranking, but the realization\n791\tof that idea as a computational process. Low’s pentagons and hexagons become semantic expansions,\n792\tphonetic neighborhoods, affordance discovery, and candidate selection.\n793\tThe search remains difficult, and many puns still resist translation, but the results suggest that Low’s\n794\tcentral insight was correct. Successful pun translation is not the preservation of a word. It is the\n795\tdiscovery of another place in the language where sound and meaning collide. Languages are full of\n796\tsuch hidden bridges. The beauty of pun translation lies in finding them\n797\t\n798\tAcknowledgements\n799\tWe thank the Data Science at Georgia Tech (DS@GT) CLEF competition group for their support. This\n800\tresearch was supported in part through research cyber-infrastructure resources and services provided by\n801\tthe Partnership for an Advanced Computing Environment (PACE) at the Georgia Institute of Technology,\n802\tAtlanta, Georgia, USA [20].\n803\t\n804\tDeclaration on Generative AI\n805\tDuring the preparation of this work, the authors used gpt-5.5 in order to generate LaTeX equations\n806\tand to check grammar and spelling. After using this tool, the authors reviewed and edited the content\n807\tas needed and take full responsibility for the publication’s content.\n808\t\n809\tReferences\n810\t[1] P. A. Low, Translating jokes and puns, Perspectives: Studies in Translatology 19 (2011) 59–70.\n811\t[2] R. Taylor, B. Herbert, M. Sana, Pun intended: Multi-agent translation of wordplay with contrastive\n812\tlearning and phonetic-semantic embeddings for clef joker 2025 task 2, in: Working Notes of CLEF\n813\t2025 – Conference and Labs of the Evaluation Forum, volume 4038 of CEUR Workshop Proceedings,\n814\tCEUR-WS.org, 2025. URL: https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-4038/paper_229.pdf.\n815\t[3] J. J. Gibson, The Ecological Approach to Visual Perception, Houghton Mifflin, Boston, 1979.\n816\t[4] T. Miller, The punster’s amanuensis: The proper place of humans and machines in the translation\n817\tof wordplay, in: Proceedings of the Human-Informed Translation and Interpreting Technology\n818\tWorkshop (HiT-IT), Incoma, 2019, 2019, pp. 57–65.\n819\t[5] Wiktionary contributors, latex, 2026. URL: https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/latex.\n820\t\n821\t\f[6] A. Savary, C. Ramisch, S. Cordeiro, F. Sangati, V. Vincze, B. QasemiZadeh, M. Candito, F. Cap,\n822\tV. Giouli, I. Stoyanova, A. Doucet, The parseme shared task on automatic identification of verbal\n823\tmultiword expressions, in: Proceedings of the 13th Workshop on Multiword Expressions (MWE\n824\t2017), Association for Computational Linguistics, 2017, pp. 31–47. URL: https://aclanthology.org/\n825\tW17-1704/. doi:10.18653/v1/W17-1704.\n826\t[7] P. Lison, J. Tiedemann, M. Kouylekov, Opensubtitles2018: Statistical rescoring of sentence alignments in large, noisy parallel corpora, in: Proceedings of the Eleventh International Conference\n827\ton Language Resources and Evaluation (LREC 2018), European Language Resources Association\n828\t(ELRA), Miyazaki, Japan, 2018.\n829\t[8] M. Honnibal, I. Montani, S. Van Landeghem, A. Boyd, spacy: Industrial-strength natural language\n830\tprocessing in python, 2020. URL: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.1212303. doi:10.5281/zenodo.\n831\t1212303.\n832\t[9] J. Chen, S. Xiao, P. Zhang, K. Luo, D. Lian, Z. Liu, M3-embedding: Multi-linguality, multifunctionality, multi-granularity text embeddings through self-knowledge distillation, in: L.-W. Ku,\n833\tA. Martins, V. Srikumar (Eds.), Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL\n834\t2024, Association for Computational Linguistics, Bangkok, Thailand, 2024, pp. 2318–2335. URL:\n835\thttps://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.137/. doi:10.18653/v1/2024.findings-acl.137.\n836\t[10] J. Johnson, M. Douze, H. Jégou, Billion-scale similarity search with gpus, arXiv preprint\n837\tarXiv:1702.08734 (2017).\n838\t[11] R. Sharma, et al., Phonetic word embeddings, arXiv e-prints (2021) Xiv–2109. Ar.\n839\t[12] N. Reimers, I. Gurevych, Making monolingual sentence embeddings multilingual using knowledge\n840\tdistillation, in: Proceedings of the 2020 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language\n841\tProcessing, Association for Computational Linguistics, 2020. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.09813.\n842\t[13] E. Grave, P. Bojanowski, P. Gupta, A. Joulin, T. Mikolov, Learning word vectors for 157 languages,\n843\tin: Proceedings of the International Conference on Language Resources and Evaluation (LREC\n844\t2018), 2018.\n845\t[14] S. Wang, et al., Mmte: Corpus and metrics for evaluating machine translation quality of metaphorical language, 2024. arXiv:2406.13698, arXiv preprint.\n846\t[15] F. Góes, et al., Crowd score: A method for the evaluation of jokes using large language model ai\n847\tvoters as judges, 2022. arXiv:2212.11214, arXiv preprint.\n848\t[16] D. G. Saari, Basic geometry of voting, Springer (1995).\n849\t[17] M. G. Kendall, A new measure of rank correlation, Biometrika 30 (1938) 81–93. doi:10.1093/\n850\tbiomet/30.1-2.81.\n851\t[18] S. Zhong, et al., Let’s think outside the box: Exploring leap-of-thought in large language models\n852\twith creative humor generation, in: Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision\n853\tand Pattern Recognition, 2024.\n854\t[19] H. Wang, et al., Innovative thinking, infinite humor: Humor research of large language models\n855\tthrough structured thought leaps, 2024. arXiv:2410.10370, arXiv preprint.\n856\t[20] PACE, Partnership for an Advanced Computing Environment (PACE), 2017. URL: http://www.\n857\tpace.gatech.edu.\n858\t\n859\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e3105a96-f361-43cd-b94d-cb181b5a772a", "uuid": "078da859-64fe-4f16-bfcf-b619176a4ada", "timestamp": "2026-08-06T08:18:58.423Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608_04299.txt", "content": "Searching for Sound-Meaning Collisions: Graph-Based\nAffordance Retrieval and Multi-Evaluator Ranking for Pun\nTranslation at CLEF 2026 JOKER Task 2\nJoker Task 2 at CLEF 2026\nRussell Taylor1,* , Adam Brikman1 and Prateek Awate1\n1\n\nGeorgia Institute of Technology, North Ave NW, Atlanta, GA 30332\n\nAbstract\n\nFifteen years ago, Low proposed that pun translators should stop searching for equivalent words and instead search\nfor new points of contact between sound and meaning. In this paper, we investigate that idea computationally.\nWe model pun translation as a process of discovery, exploration, and selection. A retrieval system searches\nsemantic and phonological neighborhoods for target-language affordances: sound–meaning bridges that may\nsupport new wordplay. Multiple language models then explore these opportunities by generating competing\ntranslations, while a multi-perspective generate-and-rank architecture selects among them.\nBeyond system development, our primary contribution is an analysis of how retrieved affordances propagate\nthrough the translation process. We find that generators actively exploit retrieved opportunities, evaluators\nprogressively concentrate around stronger sound–meaning bridges, and exact phonological collisions are selected\nat disproportionately high rates when available. At the same time, many puns still yield no usable affordances,\nsuggesting that retrieval remains the central bottleneck in computational pun translation.\nThe resulting picture is remarkably close to the process envisioned by Low. Successful pun translation emerges\nnot from preserving source-language words, but from discovering new places in the target language where sound\nand meaning collide.\n\nKeywords\n\nPun translation, Computational humor, Wordplay generation, Retrieval augmented generation\n\n1. Introduction\nPun translation is often described as impossible because the linguistic accident that creates a pun in\none language rarely survives translation into another. Yet translators have long known that successful\npun translation does not require preserving the original word. It requires finding another place in the\ntarget language where sound and meaning collide.\nOne particularly useful perspective was proposed by Low, who argued that translators should not\nsearch for equivalent words, but for new points of contact between sound and meaning. His squares,\npentagons, and hexagons describe a progressively expanding search through semantic and phonological\nneighborhoods in pursuit of such opportunities. Although developed as a practical framework for\nprofessional human translators, the underlying process resembles a search procedure that lends itself\nnaturally to computational implementation [1].\nOur 2025 CLEF Joker Task 2 submissions, which achieved first and second place in human evaluation,\nwere heavily influenced by this perspective. We combined semantic and phonetic retrieval with multiagent evaluation and found that systems optimized for humor often outperformed approaches focused\non literal translation [2]. However, an important question remained unanswered: what role does\nretrieval actually play in successful pun translation?\nIn this work we treat pun translation as a process of discovery, exploration, and selection. Retrieval\nidentifies semantic–phonetic affordances: target-language opportunities where sound and meaning may\nCLEF 2026 Working Notes, 21 – 24 September 2026, Jena, Germany\nCorresponding author.\n$ rdtaylorjr@gatech.edu (R. Taylor); abrikman3@gatech.edu (A. Brikman); pawate3@gatech.edu (P. Awate)\n\u001a 0009-0007-0702-2375 (R. Taylor); 0000-0000-0000-0000 (A. Brikman); 0009-0002-1878-9700 (P. Awate)\n\n*\n\n© 2026 Copyright for this paper by its authors. Use permitted under Creative Commons License Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0).\n\n\fsupport new wordplay [3]. Generators explore these opportunities by producing multiple competing\ntranslations. Evaluators then select among them using multiple perspectives reflecting humor, wordplay\nquality, fluency, and preservation of the joke’s underlying setup and humorous intent.\nTo support this investigation, we substantially expand the retrieval space through phrase-level\nsemantic embeddings and learned phonetic embeddings, replace regeneration-based evaluation with\na two-stage generate-and-rank architecture, and trace retrieved affordances throughout the entire\ntranslation pipeline. This allows us not only to improve performance, but also to study which sound–\nmeaning bridges survive successive stages of generation and selection.\nThe results suggest that successful pun translation is neither direct translation nor unconstrained\ncreativity. It is a search process. Retrieval uncovers hidden bridges between semantic domains, generators explore them, and evaluators progressively concentrate around the strongest collisions between\nsound and meaning.\n\n2. System Improvements\n2.1. Preprocessing\nConsistent with Miller’s proposed computational framework for pun translation, preprocessing aims to\nidentify the source pun and its associated semantic domains before retrieval and candidate generation\n[4].\n2.1.1. Data Cleaning\nWe revised our preprocessing pipeline to improve LLM comprehension of the source puns [2]. Compared\nto our previous system, the cleaner normalizes Unicode punctuation, repairs malformed quotation and\ndialogue structures, preserves lexical hashtag cues that explicitly mark pun words, removes socialmedia artifacts (URLs, handles, hashtags, emojis, and reaction text), and incorporates approximately\n100 dataset-specific repairs identified through automated validation. These changes were intended to\nimprove downstream pun identification and interpretation rather than contribute directly to translation\nquality.\n2.1.2. Pun Word Identification and Translation\nWe retained the pun word identification and translation stage from our previous system but simplified\nthe output representation. The model identifies the pun word, pun type, and two semantic domain\nword lists associated with the wordplay. Unlike the previous system, we removed explicit context-word\nextraction and translate only the semantic fields required for downstream retrieval. We implemented\nthe pipeline using gemini-3-flash and structured JSON outputs.\n\n2.2. Expanded Affordance Embedding Spaces\nA limitation of our previous system was that affordance discovery operated primarily at the word level.\nMany puns, however, rely on fixed expressions, collocations, and phrase-level reinterpretations that\nextend beyond individual lexical items. To address this limitation, we constructed a 370,450-entry\nFrench expression bank and indexed it for dense retrieval.\n2.2.1. Expression-Level Semantic Embeddings\nThe expression bank combines 51,589 French Wiktionary entries labeled as expressions, proverbs, or\nlocutions [5]; 12,308 PARSEME-derived sentence contexts containing annotated multiword expressions\n[6]; and 306,553 high-association OpenSubtitles collocations [7] mined using spaCy lemmatization,\npart-of-speech pattern filtering, frequency thresholds, and pointwise mutual information scoring [8].\nAfter normalization and deduplication, 355,803 entries (96.1%) consisted of multi-word units.\n\n\fAll entries were embedded using BAAI/bge-m3 [9] with normalized embeddings and indexed using\nFAISS inner-product search [10]. Unlike the previous system, which primarily searched for individual\nlexical affordances, the expression bank enables retrieval over multi-word expressions, collocations,\nidiomatic constructions, and longer semantic contexts. These additions expand the search space available\nto the retrieval system and provide phrase-level semantic anchors capable of supporting target-language\nwordplay.\n2.2.2. Learned Phonetic Embeddings\nStarting from a French lexicon containing orthographic forms and IPA transcriptions, we constructed\na large-scale phonetic relevance graph containing 245,746 unique word–IPA pairs and 4.46 million\nlabeled phonological relations [11]. Positive relations included exact homophones, near-homophones,\nschwa-deletion variants, rhotic variants, nasalization variants, rhyme relations, consonant-skeleton\nrelations, and vowel-skeleton relations; hard negatives were mined from phonologically similar forms\nthat lacked meaningful phonological equivalence. The resulting dataset yielded 2.72 million positive\ntraining pairs and 355,007 held-out evaluation relations. Relation counts are summarized in Table 1.\nTable 1\nPositive relation types used for phonetic embedding.\nRelation type\n\nCount\n\nStrong rhyme\nVowel skeleton\nConsonant skeleton\nRhotic variant\nNear homophone (edit distance 2)\nNasalization variant\nExact homophone\nSchwa deletion\nWeak rhyme\nNear homophone (edit distance 1)\nBoundary deletion\n\n1,518,818\n426,532\n421,482\n73,139\n66,379\n57,013\n56,521\n30,295\n25,761\n8,198\n323\n\nTotal\n\n2,684,461\n\nUnlike traditional phonetic retrieval systems that focus primarily on exact pronunciation similarity, the supervision signal intentionally emphasizes broader phonological neighborhoods, including\nrhyme, consonant-skeleton, and vowel-skeleton relations, to support retrieval of approximate sound\ncorrespondences useful for pun construction. These relations are used to fine-tune BAAI/bge-m3 [9]\non IPA sequences using SentenceTransformers and Multiple Negatives Ranking Loss [12]. Training\npairs are balanced through capped per-relation sampling to prevent dominant relation classes from\noverwhelming the embedding geometry. Training used a batch size of 128 for one epoch. The resulting\nembedding space was trained to capture phonological relationships useful for pun construction rather\nthan exact pronunciation equivalence alone. The trained model embeds IPA representations into a\ndense retrieval space indexed with FAISS [10] and supports approximate phonetic retrieval through\ncosine-similarity nearest-neighbor search.\nWe evaluated the learned phonetic encoder on an intrinsic retrieval benchmark containing 6,321\nquery IPA forms and 244,948 indexed items. Performance was strongest for near-homophone and\nconsonant-skeleton relations, achieving Recall@10 of 0.642 and 0.371 respectively, while hard-negative\nretrieval remained near zero. These results indicate that the embedding space effectively captures\npun-relevant phonetic similarity beyond exact homophone matching.\n\n\fTable 2\nIntrinsic phonetic retrieval evaluation.\nRelation\n\nR@10\n\nMRR\n\nAll\nNear homophone (edit 1)\nConsonant skeleton\nStrong rhyme\nHard negative\n\n0.394\n0.642\n0.371\n0.027\n0.000\n\n0.191\n0.293\n0.172\n0.013\n0.000\n\n2.3. Graph-Based Affordance Retrieval\nFollowing Low’s proposal of pun translation as a search for exploitable sound–meaning collisions,\nwe model retrieval as exploration of semantic and phonetic neighborhoods connecting two semantic\ndomains rather than direct translation between individual lexical items. We refer to retrieved phoneticsemantic pairs as affordances because they provide opportunities for target-language pun construction\nrather than direct translations.\nWe view the retrieval space as a heterogeneous graph whose vertices are lexical items and expressions.\nSemantic retrieval induces semantic edges, while phonetic retrieval induces phonetic edges. Affordance\ndiscovery corresponds to identifying bridge structures that connect the semantic regions associated\nwith the two source-pun domains through combinations of semantic and phonetic relations.\nLet 𝑉 denote the set of French lexical items and expressions. Semantic retrieval induces semantic\nneighborhoods 𝑁𝑆 (·), while phonetic retrieval induces phonetic neighborhoods 𝑁𝑃 (·). Let 𝐴 and 𝐵\ndenote the two translated lists of words representing the distinct semantic domains of the source pun\nword. These serve as the seed sets for two semantic regions.\n2.3.1. Semantic Expansion\nEach semantic region is expanded independently through dense retrieval over the French expression\nbank and bounded FastText lexical expansion [13]. The resulting semantic neighborhoods are\n𝐴𝑆 = 𝑁𝑆 (𝐴),\n\n𝐵𝑆 = 𝑁𝑆 (𝐵).\n\nAll retrieved candidates must possess an attested IPA representation before participating in subsequent\nphonetic retrieval. Detached phonetic recovery is defined as\n𝐴𝑃 = 𝑁𝑃 (𝐴 ∪ 𝐴𝑆 ),\n\n𝐵𝑃 = 𝑁𝑃 (𝐵 ∪ 𝐵𝑆 ),\n\nwhere 𝑁𝑃 denotes phonetic-neighborhood expansion in the learned IPA embedding space.\n2.3.2. Bucketized Bridge Mining\nWe define phonetic bridge retrieval as\nΦ(𝑋, 𝑌 ) = {(𝑥, 𝑦) ∈ 𝑋 × 𝑌 : ipa(𝑥) ̸= ∅, ipa(𝑦) ̸= ∅, cos(e𝑥 , e𝑦 ) ≥ 𝜏 }\nwhere e𝑥 and e𝑦 are the learned phonetic embeddings of 𝑥 and 𝑦, respectively. For each bucket,\nretrieval considers all candidate pairs between the source and target candidate sets and retains only\nthose satisfying the phonetic similarity criterion defined by Φ.\nRetrieval proceeds through six ordered stages:\n1. Phonetic matches between original domain terms\n𝑅1 = Φ(𝐴, 𝐵)\n\n\f2. Phonetic matches between semantic expansions of 𝐴 and original 𝐵 terms\n𝑅2 = Φ(𝐴𝑆 , 𝐵)\n3. Phonetic matches between original 𝐴 terms and semantic expansions of 𝐵\n𝑅3 = Φ(𝐴, 𝐵𝑆 )\n4. Phonetic matches between semantic expansions of both domains\n𝑅4 = Φ(𝐴𝑆 , 𝐵𝑆 )\n5. Phonetic recovery from the 𝐴 domain as the sole semantic anchor\n𝑅5 = Φ(𝐴𝑃 , 𝐴 ∪ 𝐴𝑆 )\n6. Phonetic recovery from the 𝐵 domain as the sole semantic anchor\n𝑅6 = Φ(𝐵𝑃 , 𝐵 ∪ 𝐵𝑆 )\nDuplicate affordances retain ownership of the earliest stage in which they are discovered. Phonetic\nretrieval is performed through nearest-neighbor search in the learned IPA embedding space rather\nthan exact homophone lookup, allowing retrieval of approximate sound relationships learned during\nphonetic embedding training.\n2.3.3. Affordance Ranking and Pruning\nAffordance ranking combines several heuristic quality signals. Phonetic scores measure pronunciation similarity, semantic scores measure anchorability to the source semantic domains, naturalness\nscores estimate recognizability as French lexical material, and pivot scores estimate usefulness for pun\nconstruction. Overall scores represent the combined affordance-ranking score used during retrieval\npruning.\nSeveral pruning stages are applied before export. Exact duplicates are removed. Trivial inflectional\nvariants, accent variants, plural variants, and same-root morphological echoes are penalized or filtered.\nPer-surface and per-root caps prevent a single lexical family from dominating the affordance list.\nAdditional constraints limit identity affordances and other low-creativity collisions.\nThe final retrieval output is a diversified set of higher-scoring retrieved affordances under our filtering\ncriteria, distributed across the expanded retrieval space. These affordances serve as constraints and\ninspiration for downstream French pun generation.\n\n2.4. Candidate Generation\nOur previous system generated a single French pun per input row using the English source text,\ntranslated semantic domains, and a small set of retrieved French affordances. However, affordance\nretrieval covered only 14% of the dataset, limiting the usefulness of retrieval-guided generation.\nThe expanded retrieval system increased affordance coverage to 50.8% of examples and produced\nbetween one and six affordances for each successful retrieval. This provided more frequent and diverse\nguidance to the generation stage.\nFor claude-sonnet-4.6 and gemini-3-flash, the generator received the English pun, translated\nsemantic domains, and retrieved affordances and produced twelve distinct French pun candidates. The\nprompt encouraged different forms of French wordplay, including homophony, near-homophony,\npolysemy, idiomatic reinterpretation, phrase-level wordplay, and semantic compensation. Retrieved\naffordances were presented as creative opportunities rather than required lexical targets, allowing the\nmodel to explore multiple ways of exploiting the same underlying semantic and phonetic relationships.\nFor gpt-5.5 and gemini-3.1-pro-preview, a single candidate was generated for each example\nand passed directly to the ensemble stage. These runs were included to evaluate stronger frontier\nmodels with a lower-cost of generation.\nThis shifted generation from a single-solution paradigm to a generate-and-select paradigm, allowing\ndownstream evaluators to choose among multiple realizations of the same retrieved affordance structure.\n\n\f2.5. Two-Stage Multi-Agent Ranking\nThe 2025 system evaluated a single generated pun using multiple agents that assigned scores and\ntriggered regeneration when scores fell below a threshold. In contrast, the 2026 system reformulates\nevaluation as a two-stage ranking problem.\n2.5.1. Stage 1: Intra-Model Candidate Selection\nFor claude-sonnet-4.6 and gemini-3-flash, the generator produces twelve candidate puns for\neach source example. Candidate strings are extracted, assigned deterministic five-digit identifiers,\nstripped of generator metadata, and randomly shuffled before evaluation. The evaluator therefore never\nobserves candidate order or model provenance.\nA single judge model is prompted to rank candidates from four independent perspectives [14][15]:\n• Comedian - Which candidate is funniest to a native French speaker?\n• Linguist - Which candidate exhibits the strongest and most recognizable wordplay?\n• Editor – Which candidate is most natural and fluent in French?\n• Translator – Which candidate best preserves the humorous intent of the English source?\nRather than assigning numerical scores, each persona returns an ordered top-five ranking of candidate\nidentifiers. Rankings are then aggregated using weighted Borda voting [16]. Candidates omitted from\na persona’s top-five list receive zero points. The candidate with the highest aggregate Borda score is\nselected as the Stage 1 winner. The output of Stage 1 is a single winning candidate for each generator\nmodel.\n2.5.2. Stage 2: Cross-Model Ensemble Selection\nNext, the Stage 1 winners from claude-sonnet-4.6 and gemini-3-flash are combined with singlecandidate outputs generated by gpt-5.5 and gemini-3.1-pro. This produces four candidate puns for each\nsource example.\nAs in Stage 1, candidates are assigned fresh deterministic identifiers, shuffled, and stripped of sourcemodel metadata before evaluation. The same four personas independently rank all four candidates.\nPersona rankings are again aggregated using weighted Borda voting to produce a final ensemble winner.\nThe ranking pipeline is executed using gpt-5.5, claude-sonnet-4.6, and gemini-3.1-pro as\njudge models. We evaluated multiple aggregation strategies:\n1. Judges then models - Aggregate persona rankings within each judge model, then aggregate\nacross judge models.\n2. Models then judges - Aggregate judge-model rankings first, then aggregate persona rankings.\n3. Pooled rankings - Aggregate all rankings simultaneously within a single Borda computation.\nJudge-model weights and persona weights are independently configurable, allowing ensemble variants\nto emphasize different evaluation criteria. We additionally measure inter-judge agreement, promptposition exposure effects, and model self-preference using normal, half-weight, and removed-self-vote\ncorrection schemes.\n\n3. Results\n3.1. Retrieval Statistics\nThe retrieval system generated 3,748 affordances for 2,064 of 4,061 source puns, corresponding to 50.8%\ncoverage (Table 3). Covered source puns contained an average of 1.82 affordances, while the overall\naverage was 0.92 affordances per source pun. Coverage was slightly higher for homophonic puns (55.1%)\nthan for homographic puns (50.2%).\n\n\fDirect 𝐴𝑆 –𝐵𝑆 bridges accounted for 2,252 retrieved affordances (60.1% of the total). Detached\nphonetic recovery contributed the remaining 1,496 affordances, split between 𝐴𝑃 -anchored retrieval\n(719 affordances; 19.2%) and 𝐵𝑃 -anchored retrieval (777 affordances; 20.7%) (Table 3).\nTable 3\nRetrieval output by bucket.\nRetrieval bucket\n\nSource puns\n\nAffordances\n\nMean score\n\n𝐴𝑆 –𝐵𝑆 bridge\n𝐴𝑃 -anchored retrieval\n𝐵𝑃 -anchored retrieval\n\n1,287 (31.7%)\n556 (13.7%)\n587 (14.5%)\n\n2,252 (60.1%)\n719 (19.2%)\n777 (20.7%)\n\n0.711\n0.626\n0.626\n\nTotal\n\n2,064 (50.8%)\n\n3,748 (100.0%)\n\n0.677\n\nThe retrieval inventory was dominated by near-homophonic bridges. Similar-sound relations accounted for 3,344 affordances (89.2%), while same-sound relations accounted for 404 affordances (10.8%).\nSame-sound affordances achieved higher mean overall scores than similar-sound affordances (0.817\nvs. 0.660). Likewise, bridges discovered between 𝐴𝑆 and 𝐵𝑆 achieved higher mean overall scores than\ndetached recovery affordances (0.711 vs. 0.626).\nThe affordance count distribution was strongly skewed toward small candidate sets (Table 4). Nearly\nhalf of all source puns produced no affordances, while 41.2% produced one or two affordances. Only\n9.6% of source puns produced three or more affordances.\nTable 4\nDistribution of retrieved affordances per source pun.\nRetrieved affordances\n\nSource puns\n\n0\n1\n2\n3-6\n\n1,997 (49.2%)\n956 (23.5%)\n717 (17.7%)\n391 (9.6%)\n\nTotal\n\n4,061 (100.0%)\n\n3.2. Affordance Utilization\nTables 5 and 6 summarize affordance retention and quality across retrieval, generation, and selection\nstages. Successive selection stages reduced the number of represented affordances while increasing\ntheir mean quality scores.\nTable 5\nAffordance retention across retrieval, generation, and selection stages.\nStage\nAffordance retrieval\nStage 1 generation\nStage 1 selection\nStage 2 finalists\nStage 2 winners\n\nPuns using affordances\n\nDistinct affordances used\n\n2,064 (50.8%)\n1,935 (47.6%)\n1,167 (28.7%)\n443 (10.9%)\n141 (3.5%)\n\n3,748 (100.0%)\n3,335 (89.5%)\n1,832 (49.1%)\n650 (17.4%)\n206 (5.5%)\n\nAffordance usage remained dominated by 𝐴𝑆 –𝐵𝑆 bridges throughout the pipeline. These accounted\nfor 60.1% of retrieved affordances, 71.8% and 68.7% of affordance usage in claude-sonnet-4.6 and\ngemini-3-flash candidates, 73.1% and 70.4% after Stage 1 selection, and 72.7% and 72.5% among\nStage 2 finalists and winners.\n\n\fTable 6\nMean quality scores for affordances used across retrieval, generation, and selection stages. Higher values indicate\nstronger affordances.\nStage\n\nOverall\n\nPhonetic\n\nNaturalness\n\nSemantic\n\nPivot\n\nAffordance retrieval\nStage 1 generation\nStage 1 selection\nStage 2 finalists\nStage 2 winners\n\n0.677\n0.702\n0.713\n0.716\n0.724\n\n0.846\n0.858\n0.866\n0.866\n0.873\n\n0.520\n0.556\n0.572\n0.578\n0.588\n\n0.892\n0.941\n0.954\n0.968\n0.978\n\n0.520\n0.562\n0.579\n0.585\n0.596\n\nIn contrast, same-sound affordances were consistently overrepresented relative to their prevalence in\nthe retrieval inventory. Although they comprised only 10.8% of retrieved affordances, they accounted\nfor 17.6% and 14.9% of affordance usage in claude-sonnet-4.6 and gemini-3-flash candidates,\n22.9% and 19.4% after Stage 1 selection, and 23.8% and 27.5% among Stage 2 finalists and winners.\nAmong candidates that used retrieval, claude-sonnet-4.6 candidates employed an average of\n1.38 affordances and gemini-3-flash candidates employed an average of 1.36 affordances. Ranked\ncandidates showed similar values (1.43 and 1.39, respectively). Most affordance usage involved only\none side of a retrieved bridge. In Claude-generated candidates, 48.4% of matches used only the left\naffordance, 25.0% used only the right affordance, and 26.6% used both bridge endpoints; the corresponding proportions for gemini-3-flash were similar. Candidates that used retrieval employed 1.38\naffordances on average for claude-sonnet-4.6 and 1.36 for gemini-3-flash, increasing to 1.43\nand 1.39 after Stage 1 selection.\n\n3.3. Candidate Selection Results\nThe judge personas exhibited substantial diversity in their preferred candidates. Complete agreement occurred for only 16.8% of Claude-generated examples and 5.4% of Gemini-generated examples. Claude examples produced an average of 2.29 distinct persona winners, compared with 2.63 for gemini-3-flash.\nMore than half of gemini-3-flash examples (56.9%) and 38.7% of claude-sonnet-4.6 examples\nproduced at least three distinct persona winners, indicating that different personas frequently prioritized\ndifferent candidate qualities. This diversity of preferences motivates the use of rank aggregation rather\nthan relying on a single evaluation perspective.\n\n3.4. Ensemble Results\nDiscriminator models exhibited markedly different selection preferences. The gemini-3.1-pro judge\nselected Gemini-family candidates 72.4% of the time, the gpt-5.5 judge selected GPT-generated\ncandidates 32.6% of the time, and the claude-sonnet-4.6 judge selected Claude-generated candidates\nas its top-ranked choice 27.1% of the time. As shown in Table 8, discriminator choice and persona\nweighting both substantially affected the final winner distribution, with gemini-3.1-pro judging\nstrongly self-biased and translator-only weighting increasing the share of gpt-5.5 winners.\nTable 7\nGenerator sources selected under different Stage 2 aggregation orders.\nAggregation\n\ngpt-5.5\n\ngemini-3.1-pro\n\nclaude-sonnet-4.6\n\ngemini-3-flash\n\nJudges-first\nModels-first\nPooled\n\n1,165\n1,161\n1,176\n\n1,954\n1,993\n2,066\n\n507\n463\n459\n\n435\n444\n360\n\nPersona disagreement remained common throughout cross-model selection. Complete persona\nagreement on the top candidate occurred in only 10.6% for gpt-5.5 judging, 25% for gemini-3.1-pro\n\n\fjudging, and 18.0% of examples for claude-sonnet-4.6 judging. Most examples produced two or\nthree distinct persona winners. Despite disagreement at rank one, personas generally ranked similar\ncandidate sets highly, with all four finalists appearing in every persona’s top-four ranking. Pairwise\nKendall rank correlations ranged from 0.56 to 0.86 depending on the persona pair [17].\nTable 8\nStage 2 winner distributions under selected persona-weighting and discriminator-model configurations. Entries\nreport final winner counts by generator model, with percentages of all 4,061 puns.\nPersona weights\n\nDiscriminator models\n\ngpt-5.5\n\ngemini-3.1-pro\n\nclaude-sonnet-4.6\n\ngemini-3-flash\n\n25/25/25/25\n45/30/25/10\nComedian\nLinguist\nEditor\nTranslator\n\nAll (pooled)\nAll (pooled)\nAll (pooled)\nAll (pooled)\nAll (pooled)\nAll (pooled)\n\n1,176 (29.0%)\n1,003 (24.7%)\n944 (23.2%)\n1,053 (25.9%)\n810 (19.9%)\n1,735 (42.7%)\n\n2,066 (50.9%)\n1,847 (45.5%)\n1,549 (38.1%)\n1,623 (40.0%)\n1,886 (46.4%)\n1,793 (44.2%)\n\n459 (11.3%)\n517 (12.7%)\n678 (16.7%)\n471 (11.6%)\n626 (15.4%)\n459 (11.3%)\n\n360 (8.9%)\n694 (17.1%)\n890 (21.9%)\n914 (22.5%)\n739 (18.2%)\n74 (1.8%)\n\n25/25/25/25\n45/30/25/10\nComedian\nLinguist\nEditor\nTranslator\n\ngpt-5.5\ngpt-5.5\ngpt-5.5\ngpt-5.5\ngpt-5.5\ngpt-5.5\n\n1,418 (34.9%)\n1,166 (28.7%)\n1,101 (27.1%)\n1,175 (28.9%)\n888 (21.9%)\n2,126 (52.4%)\n\n1,630 (40.1%)\n1,441 (35.5%)\n1,310 (32.3%)\n1,404 (34.6%)\n1,602 (39.4%)\n1,409 (34.7%)\n\n483 (11.9%)\n541 (13.3%)\n656 (16.2%)\n461 (11.4%)\n663 (16.3%)\n462 (11.4%)\n\n530 (13.1%)\n913 (22.5%)\n994 (24.5%)\n1,021 (25.1%)\n908 (22.4%)\n64 (1.6%)\n\n25/25/25/25\n45/30/25/10\nComedian\nLinguist\nEditor\nTranslator\n\ngemini-3.1-pro\ngemini-3.1-pro\ngemini-3.1-pro\ngemini-3.1-pro\ngemini-3.1-pro\ngemini-3.1-pro\n\n737 (18.1%)\n637 (15.7%)\n640 (15.8%)\n668 (16.4%)\n622 (15.3%)\n1,188 (29.3%)\n\n2,638 (65.0%)\n2,358 (58.1%)\n2,121 (52.2%)\n2,123 (52.3%)\n2,290 (56.4%)\n2,476 (61.0%)\n\n267 (6.6%)\n303 (7.5%)\n409 (10.1%)\n304 (7.5%)\n374 (9.2%)\n300 (7.4%)\n\n419 (10.3%)\n763 (18.8%)\n891 (21.9%)\n966 (23.8%)\n775 (19.1%)\n97 (2.4%)\n\n25/25/25/25\n45/30/25/10\nComedian\nLinguist\nEditor\nTranslator\n\nclaude-sonnet-4.6\nclaude-sonnet-4.6\nclaude-sonnet-4.6\nclaude-sonnet-4.6\nclaude-sonnet-4.6\nclaude-sonnet-4.6\n\n1,238 (30.5%)\n1,048 (25.8%)\n890 (21.9%)\n1,191 (29.3%)\n950 (23.4%)\n1,450 (35.7%)\n\n1,179 (29.0%)\n1,026 (25.3%)\n868 (21.4%)\n873 (21.5%)\n1,301 (32.0%)\n1,391 (34.3%)\n\n1,100 (27.1%)\n1,192 (29.4%)\n1,364 (33.6%)\n1,013 (24.9%)\n1,059 (26.1%)\n979 (24.1%)\n\n544 (13.4%)\n795 (19.6%)\n939 (23.1%)\n984 (24.2%)\n751 (18.5%)\n241 (5.9%)\n\n3.5. Shared Task Results\nAt the time of writing, our best submission is ranked first on the public leaderboard with a score of\n37.783. Our strongest single-model system is ranked second (37.119), while the best all-model translator\nensemble is ranked third (35.949).\nThe leaderboard ablations show three clear trends. First, GPT-based generation outperformed\nthe other generator families: gpt-5.5 single (37.119) exceeded gemini-3.1-pro single (30.250),\nclaude-sonnet-4.6 translator (29.547), and gemini-3-flash translator (23.029). Second, translatorfocused selection consistently outperformed other persona weightings. The best translator ensemble\nachieved 37.783, compared with 25.761 for editor-only, 24.161 for pun-expert-only, and 24.044 for\ncomedian-only selection. Third, candidate pooling was beneficial only when combined with translatororiented ranking. The all-pooled translator ensemble achieved 35.949, compared with 28.884 for the\nequal-weight ensemble, 28.493 for judges-first aggregation, and 28.259 for models-first aggregation.\nEnsemble weighting also had a large effect. Translator-focused weighting outperformed comedianand pun-focused weighting schemes. This result is consistent with our 2025 findings, where systems\noptimized for semantic fidelity achieved higher BLEU and BERTScore despite often producing weaker\ntarget-language wordplay. The official evaluation metric therefore appears to reward preservation\nof source meaning more strongly than creative pun generation, creating a potential tension between\nleaderboard optimization and humor quality.\nHuman evaluation, which is the primary target of our system design, has not yet been performed,\nand not all participating submissions are visible on the leaderboard. The camera-ready version will\nreport final leaderboard standings and official human evaluation results.\n\n\f4. Discussion\n4.1. Retrieval actually matters\nRetrieved affordances were actively exploited by generators and progressively concentrated among\nselected winners. As affordance diversity decreased across successive pipeline stages, the mean quality\nof surviving affordances increased. This suggests that generation and ranking preferentially retained\nstronger semantic–phonetic collisions rather than treating all retrieved affordances equally.\n\n4.2. Retrieval is still the bottleneck\nDespite tripling retrieval coverage, nearly half of source puns still yielded no affordances, and retrieved\naffordances appeared in only 3.5% of final ensemble winners. This highlights the central challenge\nof retrieval-guided pun translation: useful sound–meaning collisions are both rare and difficult to\ndiscover. Rather than serving as a pervasive generation mechanism, affordances functioned as sparse\nbut valuable opportunities that were exploited only when the retrieval system uncovered particularly\nstrong semantic–phonetic bridges. This difficulty mirrors the challenges faced by human translators,\nwho frequently abandon source-side wordplay when no suitable target-language collision exists.\n\n4.3. Same-sound affordances are disproportionately successful\nAlthough exact sound matches constituted only a small fraction of retrieved affordances, they became\nincreasingly overrepresented among selected candidates. Approximate phonetic similarity appears\nvaluable for exploration, but exact phonological collisions are selected at disproportionately high rates\nwhen available. This pattern suggests that successful target-language wordplay remains concentrated\naround rare exact sound correspondences, which provide particularly strong and easily recoverable\nopportunities for humorous reinterpretation.\n\n4.4. Affordances cluster around a small number of strong bridges\nAlthough retrieval produced thousands of affordances, many represented variations of the same underlying semantic–phonetic opportunity. Successful pun translation therefore appears to depend less on the\ntotal number of retrieved affordances than on the number of distinct semantic–phonetic bridges available for exploitation. Future work should focus on exploring more distant semantic and phonological\nneighborhoods capable of surfacing genuinely novel opportunities for wordplay [18][19].\n\n4.5. The persistence of position bias\nCandidate order influenced selection behavior even after randomization, indicating that current LLM\njudges are not fully position invariant. Reducing these effects will likely require repeated evaluation\nunder multiple candidate orderings, albeit at increased computational cost. The persistence of position\neffects despite randomization suggests that ranking variance remains a significant source of uncertainty\nin LLM-based evaluation pipelines.\n\n4.6. Healthy disagreement is a good thing\nStrong disagreement between judge personas indicates that pun quality is inherently multi-objective.\nHumor, wordplay strength, fluency, and translation fidelity frequently favored different candidates,\nsuggesting that multi-perspective evaluation captures aspects of quality that would be lost under a\nsingle evaluator objective.\nSimilarly, Gemini-3.1-pro exhibited substantial self-preference, whereas the other judges displayed\nweaker generator-specific preferences. Multi-judge aggregation therefore reduces reliance on any single\ndiscriminator’s biases.\n\n\f4.7. What the leaderboard is actually rewarding\nTranslator-oriented ranking strategies substantially outperformed comedian-, editor-, and pun-focused\nalternatives on the public leaderboard. This large performance gap suggests that current automatic\nmetrics underweight the qualities that make target-language puns entertaining to human readers\nand instead place greater emphasis on preservation of source meaning. Notably, the strongest singlemodel result was obtained by a single gpt-5.5 generation rather than a generate-and-select pipeline,\nsuggesting that generator capability remains a major determinant of leaderboard performance. Human\nevaluation will therefore be necessary to determine whether leaderboard gains correspond to genuine\nimprovements in translated wordplay.\n\n5. Conclusion\nOur analyses suggest that successful pun translation emerges through a process of discovery, exploration,\nand selection. Retrieval uncovers semantic–phonetic affordances, generators explore the opportunities\nthey create, and evaluators progressively concentrate around the strongest sound–meaning collisions.\nThis picture is remarkably close to the account proposed by Low, who argued that translators need\nnot search for equivalent words, but for new points of contact between meaning and sound. In that\nsense, the most satisfying outcome of this work is not any particular score or ranking, but the realization\nof that idea as a computational process. Low’s pentagons and hexagons become semantic expansions,\nphonetic neighborhoods, affordance discovery, and candidate selection.\nThe search remains difficult, and many puns still resist translation, but the results suggest that Low’s\ncentral insight was correct. Successful pun translation is not the preservation of a word. It is the\ndiscovery of another place in the language where sound and meaning collide. Languages are full of\nsuch hidden bridges. The beauty of pun translation lies in finding them\n\nAcknowledgements\nWe thank the Data Science at Georgia Tech (DS@GT) CLEF competition group for their support. This\nresearch was supported in part through research cyber-infrastructure resources and services provided by\nthe Partnership for an Advanced Computing Environment (PACE) at the Georgia Institute of Technology,\nAtlanta, Georgia, USA [20].\n\nDeclaration on Generative AI\nDuring the preparation of this work, the authors used gpt-5.5 in order to generate LaTeX equations\nand to check grammar and spelling. After using this tool, the authors reviewed and edited the content\nas needed and take full responsibility for the publication’s content.\n\nReferences\n[1] P. A. Low, Translating jokes and puns, Perspectives: Studies in Translatology 19 (2011) 59–70.\n[2] R. Taylor, B. Herbert, M. Sana, Pun intended: Multi-agent translation of wordplay with contrastive\nlearning and phonetic-semantic embeddings for clef joker 2025 task 2, in: Working Notes of CLEF\n2025 – Conference and Labs of the Evaluation Forum, volume 4038 of CEUR Workshop Proceedings,\nCEUR-WS.org, 2025. URL: https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-4038/paper_229.pdf.\n[3] J. J. Gibson, The Ecological Approach to Visual Perception, Houghton Mifflin, Boston, 1979.\n[4] T. Miller, The punster’s amanuensis: The proper place of humans and machines in the translation\nof wordplay, in: Proceedings of the Human-Informed Translation and Interpreting Technology\nWorkshop (HiT-IT), Incoma, 2019, 2019, pp. 57–65.\n[5] Wiktionary contributors, latex, 2026. URL: https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/latex.\n\n\f[6] A. Savary, C. Ramisch, S. Cordeiro, F. Sangati, V. Vincze, B. QasemiZadeh, M. Candito, F. Cap,\nV. Giouli, I. Stoyanova, A. Doucet, The parseme shared task on automatic identification of verbal\nmultiword expressions, in: Proceedings of the 13th Workshop on Multiword Expressions (MWE\n2017), Association for Computational Linguistics, 2017, pp. 31–47. URL: https://aclanthology.org/\nW17-1704/. doi:10.18653/v1/W17-1704.\n[7] P. Lison, J. Tiedemann, M. Kouylekov, Opensubtitles2018: Statistical rescoring of sentence alignments in large, noisy parallel corpora, in: Proceedings of the Eleventh International Conference\non Language Resources and Evaluation (LREC 2018), European Language Resources Association\n(ELRA), Miyazaki, Japan, 2018.\n[8] M. Honnibal, I. Montani, S. Van Landeghem, A. Boyd, spacy: Industrial-strength natural language\nprocessing in python, 2020. URL: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.1212303. doi:10.5281/zenodo.\n1212303.\n[9] J. Chen, S. Xiao, P. Zhang, K. Luo, D. Lian, Z. Liu, M3-embedding: Multi-linguality, multifunctionality, multi-granularity text embeddings through self-knowledge distillation, in: L.-W. Ku,\nA. Martins, V. Srikumar (Eds.), Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL\n2024, Association for Computational Linguistics, Bangkok, Thailand, 2024, pp. 2318–2335. URL:\nhttps://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.137/. doi:10.18653/v1/2024.findings-acl.137.\n[10] J. Johnson, M. Douze, H. Jégou, Billion-scale similarity search with gpus, arXiv preprint\narXiv:1702.08734 (2017).\n[11] R. Sharma, et al., Phonetic word embeddings, arXiv e-prints (2021) Xiv–2109. Ar.\n[12] N. Reimers, I. Gurevych, Making monolingual sentence embeddings multilingual using knowledge\ndistillation, in: Proceedings of the 2020 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language\nProcessing, Association for Computational Linguistics, 2020. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.09813.\n[13] E. Grave, P. Bojanowski, P. Gupta, A. Joulin, T. Mikolov, Learning word vectors for 157 languages,\nin: Proceedings of the International Conference on Language Resources and Evaluation (LREC\n2018), 2018.\n[14] S. Wang, et al., Mmte: Corpus and metrics for evaluating machine translation quality of metaphorical language, 2024. arXiv:2406.13698, arXiv preprint.\n[15] F. Góes, et al., Crowd score: A method for the evaluation of jokes using large language model ai\nvoters as judges, 2022. arXiv:2212.11214, arXiv preprint.\n[16] D. G. Saari, Basic geometry of voting, Springer (1995).\n[17] M. G. Kendall, A new measure of rank correlation, Biometrika 30 (1938) 81–93. doi:10.1093/\nbiomet/30.1-2.81.\n[18] S. Zhong, et al., Let’s think outside the box: Exploring leap-of-thought in large language models\nwith creative humor generation, in: Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision\nand Pattern Recognition, 2024.\n[19] H. Wang, et al., Innovative thinking, infinite humor: Humor research of large language models\nthrough structured thought leaps, 2024. arXiv:2410.10370, arXiv preprint.\n[20] PACE, Partnership for an Advanced Computing Environment (PACE), 2017. URL: http://www.\npace.gatech.edu.\n\n\f", "numLines": 859, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 859}}}
